 first job as a resident in  neurology  my work is my life it's my whole life i  was just completely living  i can't say whether i want to go back to  syria  and live there or stay here forever  [Applause]  maybe going back and forth will be  possible  my hope is that my children can come to  me  after all that's what i'm fighting for  because i can't go back  and let's get more on the situation for  migrants in germany we are joined now by  katarina  she is the head of the department for  education and family at the german  institute for economic research in  berlin the institute has published  several studies  assessing migration to germany welcome  to the program and thank you so much for  joining us  in our report there we just heard about  the very different experiences  of two migrants who came to germany do  they tie in  with what you have learned from other  migrants  well the perception of migrants is very  different and therefore  in our reports we usually go on  different aspects  some of the experience which we heard  from the two  refugees who came to germany  fit what we find in particular the  well-being which is really  decreasing if children if the children  are still abroad this is something what  we find in various studies that family  reunification is a very important thing  for the well-being of refugees on the  other hand we  also find if we look on this example of  this man from syria who is a doctor  that refugees once they come to germany  have relatively high expectations  concerning their employment in germany  and what we find is that not all of  these expectations could be really mac  in particular  yeah in particular women and  and refugees with uh  mentally health problems they usually  can't met their  expectations okay so reunification  um meeting the expectations when it  comes to employment  what are the other issues that you know  migrants refugees  still encounter in the country  well we also looked on children and  adolescents as one example  so one thing what i think is very  positive  is that the children and as well  adolescents who are  at school actually have a very strong  belonging  to their school this belonging is even  stronger  than what we find for other  students in germany but on the other  hand what we also find  that they are underrepresented  underrepresented  in sport clubs or in other  extracurricular activities  activities which would help them  actually  to be better integrated into the german  society  yeah sorry apologies uh back in 2015  um we know that german chancellor angela  merkel told the country we can do this  looking back was she right  i think we saw a lot of  fields and aspects where i would say we  are on a good way  but i would say after three years and  this is what our reports are looking at  three years  after the huge uh immigration into  germany  it's we are not at the end of the  integration process  so we have uh reached a lot  but there are still areas where we still  have to do  more efforts to integrate in particular  female refugees to  to really get good education for  children and adolescents  and also for uh some other  adult refugees so i would say  um partly yes  but we shouldn't um  we we should continue with all our  efforts because it's still too early  to say well we managed it thank you so  much for joining us to share that  assessment  katarina spies head of the department  for education  and family at the german institute for  economic research  we appreciate it
 

